WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.500
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:09.480
Today we are going to talk about the&nbsp;
incredible things NVIDIA showcased at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:15.300
their recent keynote at SIGGRAPH, the world’s&nbsp;
premier computer graphics conference. Now, I am&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:20.700
a computer graphics researcher by trade, I work&nbsp;
in light transport simulations, in other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:27.120
ray tracing, so many of the things that you&nbsp;
will see here today makes me very, very happy.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:33.000
For instance, this is a showcase of what&nbsp;
their Omniverse system can do now. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:39.180
tool is for building virtual worlds. It can&nbsp;
do a lot, from storing volumetric effects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:44.400
to simulating them, to animating&nbsp;
robot hands, materials, animation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:50.880
high-quality geometry and hair simulations&nbsp;
too. The incredible scenes that you see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:57.240
here were built by USD composer, this is an&nbsp;
Omniverse app to build large-scale scenes.

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:03.120
And now, hold on to your papers Fellow Scholars,&nbsp;
because some of these virtual worlds are becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:10.140
incredibly convincing. Now what does that even&nbsp;
mean? Well, I talked about simulations that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:17.340
almost indistinguishable from reality in a little&nbsp;
paper not so long ago, yes, what you see here is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:23.160
a computer simulation. Not reality. The link to&nbsp;
the paper is available in the video description.

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:27.060
So, today we ask, can scientists at NVIDIA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:31.620
perform something similar? How&nbsp;
good are their virtual worlds?

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:39.180
So let’s see what they have here. Number&nbsp;
one, OpenUSD. USD stands for universal scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:45.120
descriptor, and this supports creating virtual&nbsp;
worlds in way such that visual effects people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:53.400
designers, engineers and roboticists can work&nbsp;
together easily. For instance, this is a virtual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:59.880
car that is almost the same as the real car&nbsp;
it represents and it is assembled from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:06.300
smallest individual parts. Then, artists can&nbsp;
see what the car would look like in reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:12.000
create new variants of it, create a gorgeous&nbsp;
backdrop where they would showcase this car,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:17.640
try different environments, and it is&nbsp;
even possible to take it for a ride.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:22.560
And what really blows my mind is&nbsp;
not only that all this is possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:30.720
but that all this is possible today in real time.&nbsp;
Everything runs as we are looking at it. Wow.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:35.640
Number two, now, if this system can&nbsp;
create really detailed geometry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:41.220
that is great. However, not everyone is a CAD&nbsp;
designer, not everyone can model, or understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:48.780
materials as a physicist does, but everyone can&nbsp;
speak English. So, they have this new interface&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:54.180
where all we need to know is English,&nbsp;
and thus, we can now get a 3D floorplan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:59.880
generate lighting through the free and&nbsp;
open-source Blender, and through Adobe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:06.480
Firefly integration, we can get floor textures&nbsp;
by just typing. I feel like we are going towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:11.760
a future where English is going to be the&nbsp;
interface of almost everything. You won’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:17.280
need to be an expert at everything, you will&nbsp;
have an expert AI assistant at your fingertips&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:24.000
with a ton of knowledge and infinite patience&nbsp;
ready to work for you. How amazing is that?

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:31.740
And you can simulate not only how things&nbsp;
look, but how things work too. Incredible.

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:38.460
Number three, they also showed us something that&nbsp;
they call Workbench. This enables fine-tuning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:43.320
already existing AI models by giving&nbsp;
them additional knowledge. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:49.920
with a text to image model, if we are asking&nbsp;
for a Toy Jensen in outer space, we get this.&nbsp;

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:56.220
Wait a minute…that’s not the Jensen we&nbsp;
are looking for is it? Now, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:01.800
this is because this model has never heard&nbsp;
of Jensen before, so we can teach it about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:10.920
him using these 8 images, then ask again,&nbsp;
and…bam! There we go! Into outer space we go!

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:16.560
Of course, they have also showcased&nbsp;
shiny new hardware. Well, mostly shiny.

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:23.160
And finally, my favorite. If I hadn’t seen&nbsp;
anything else, just this one thing you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:28.800
seeing here, that would have been enough for me&nbsp;
to create a video about this keynote. This is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:34.440
video honoring some of the incredible works in&nbsp;
computer graphics, back from the olden times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:41.640
with the incredible animated human hand back&nbsp;
from 1972 from the legendary Ed Catmull,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:48.900
co-founder of Pixar, and the first ray tracing&nbsp;
program from Turner Whitted from 1979. Imagine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:58.440
simulating refractions on a computer more than 40&nbsp;
years ago. Wow. And up to what we are capable of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:05.400
today. I am so happy to be able to share all&nbsp;
this with you, and just imagine what we will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:11.280
be capable of just two more papers down the&nbsp;
line. My goodness. What a time to be alive!

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:03.720
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

